          <a href="#prevent" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Prevention</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Oil stain removers fall into four families: absorbents that soak up free oil, alkaline degreasers that emulsify it, poultices that pull it out of the pores, and enzyme cleaners that digest it in place. Most disappointing results come from using the right family on the wrong stain, such as a degreaser on a five-year-old garage spot or an enzyme product on a puddle that needed kitty litter first. The picks below are ranked by how well each one handles the stain it was designed for, based on manufacturer instructions, label claims, product chemistry and consistent patterns in owner feedback.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Seven Concrete Oil Removers, Ranked by Stain Age
          </h2>

          <p>
            We ranked these on what the manufacturer says each product does, how its chemistry matches the stain type it targets, label safety and surface guidance, and recurring themes in owner reviews. We did not run our own lab tests. Prices are approximate ranges at the time of writing.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="four-families" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Four Families of Oil Stain Removers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every product on the shelf fits into one of four groups, and knowing which group you are buying explains most of the difference in results. <strong>Absorbents</strong> such as clay granules, diatomaceous earth and sawdust physically soak up free oil. They are cheap and safe on any surface but only help while oil is still liquid and near the top.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Degreasers</strong> are alkaline or surfactant cleaners that emulsify oil so water can carry it away. They come as concentrates or ready-to-use sprays and work quickly on fresh and recent stains. Their weakness is reach: a degreaser only works on what it touches during a 5 to 15 minute dwell, so it cleans the top fraction of an inch and leaves deeper oil behind.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Poultices</strong> combine an absorbent powder with a liquid that dissolves oil. As the liquid evaporates, it moves upward through the pores and carries oil into the powder. Commercial versions such as Pour-N-Restore make this easy, and you can mix your own from diatomaceous earth and degreaser. Poultices reach deeper than degreasers and are the go-to method for stains weeks or months old.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Enzyme and microbial cleaners</strong> introduce microbes that break hydrocarbons down into carbon dioxide and water. They keep working in the pores after you walk away, which makes them the best choice for old, faded stains and large areas. They are slow, need moisture and warmth, and are generally the gentlest option for asphalt and landscaping nearby.
          </p>

