        <div id="match-stain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Match the Product to the Stain You Actually Have
          </h2>

          <p>
            Look at the stain before you shop. A wet, shiny spot with oil you can feel is fresh, and the best purchase is an absorbent plus a degreaser. Put down granules immediately, grind them in, leave them overnight, then scrub what remains using Oil Eater, Chomp! or a similar degreaser. On fresh spills this combination commonly leaves nothing visible.
          </p>

          <p>
            A dark, dull stain that is no longer slick has been there a while. Clean it once with a degreaser to remove the surface film and let it dry. If it fades substantially, repeat. If it barely changes, the oil is below the reach of a degreaser and you need a poultice. Pour-N-Restore or a homemade diatomaceous earth poultice will often take a stain like this most of the way out in two or three rounds.
          </p>

          <p>
            A large, faded halo on a garage floor or under a long-term parking spot is old oil spread through the slab. Here an enzyme cleaner such as Terminator-HSD is the efficient choice, because covering a four-foot stain with poultice paste uses a lot of material and still only pulls from the top half inch. Apply the enzyme product, keep the area damp, and give it a week or more before judging.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, if you have many stains plus general grime and you want the whole slab to look uniform afterward, budget for a whole-surface cleaner such as Zep Driveway and Concrete Cleaner. Spot treatments tend to leave clean patches that look lighter than the surrounding concrete. Our roundup of the <a href="/best-driveway-cleaner/">best driveway cleaners</a> covers products for that broader job, including rust and mildew.
          </p>

          <p>
            A useful shortcut: press a paper towel onto the stain and stand on it for a minute. If it comes up with an oily ring, there is still free oil near the surface, and absorbent plus degreaser should be your first move. If the towel comes up clean but the stain is dark, the oil has moved into the slab, and you can skip straight to a poultice or an enzyme product instead of spending an afternoon scrubbing with a cleaner that cannot reach it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Get the Most From Any Remover
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most failed stain jobs fail on technique, not product. Start by removing free oil. Even if the stain looks dry, a quick pass with absorbent catches residual oil sitting in the surface texture. Then pre-wet the concrete lightly before applying a degreaser. Dry concrete absorbs the cleaner instantly, carrying some of the emulsified oil deeper; a damp surface keeps the action near the top where you can rinse it away.
          </p>

          <p>
            Agitation matters more than most labels admit. A stiff nylon or polypropylene brush on a pole lets you scrub with your weight behind it, which forces cleaner into the pores and breaks the oil film. Avoid wire brushes on concrete with a decorative finish and on asphalt, where they scratch and loosen stone. Keep the cleaner wet throughout its dwell time, misting it in sun or wind, because a degreaser that dries on the surface stops working and can leave residue.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rinse thoroughly. A pressure washer with a 25-degree tip or a surface cleaner attachment flushes emulsified oil out of texture better than a hose. Our guide to the <a href="/best-surface-cleaner-for-pressure-washer/">best surface cleaners for pressure washers</a> explains why a rotating cleaner leaves a more even result than a wand. Then let the concrete dry for 24 to 48 hours before deciding whether to repeat, because wet concrete hides stains.
          </p>

          <p>
            For poultices and enzymes, the rules change. Poultices need to dry slowly, so cover them with plastic and leave them a day or two. Enzymes need moisture and no rinsing for the time on the label. Neither benefits from heavy scrubbing once applied. For step-by-step methods for deep stains, see our guide on <a href="/how-to-get-oil-out-of-concrete/">how to get oil out of concrete</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="asphalt-vs-concrete" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Concrete Products on Asphalt: What Is Safe
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many products marketed as driveway oil removers assume concrete. Asphalt is a different material: its binder is a heavy petroleum product, and solvents that dissolve motor oil also soften the pavement. Before using any remover on blacktop, look for an explicit asphalt statement on the label. Water-based degreasers, enzyme cleaners and absorbents are generally safe; solvent and citrus solvent products are not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Of the picks above, Terminator-HSD and Oil-Dri granules are the most clearly asphalt-friendly because they involve no solvents. Pour-N-Restore lists asphalt on its label. For the alkaline degreasers, follow the label and test a corner first, and keep contact times short. Highly alkaline products like Purple Power are better reserved for concrete.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pressure is a second asphalt concern. A narrow tip at close range will strip fine aggregate and leave a pale, rough patch. Use a wide tip, keep a foot of distance, and keep the wand moving. If oil has already softened a spot, no cleaner will restore it; clean it, prime with an oil spot primer, and seal. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-remove-oil-stains-from-driveway/">removing driveway oil stains on asphalt and concrete</a> covers the blacktop method in full.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pavers sit between the two. Concrete pavers handle concrete products, but the sand joints between them soak up oil and wash out under high pressure. Treat both the paver and the joint, rinse gently, and top up joint sand afterward if needed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Safety, Ventilation and Runoff
          </h2>

          <p>
            Alkaline degreasers are caustic in concentrate. Wear chemical-resistant gloves and safety glasses, especially when mixing or spraying overhead, and keep them away from pets and children until the area is rinsed and dry. Read the label for surfaces to avoid; aluminum, painted trim and some sealers can be discolored by strong alkaline cleaners.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you mix a solvent poultice with acetone or mineral spirits, treat it as a fire hazard. Open the garage door, keep away from water heater and furnace pilots, and do not smoke. Never mix cleaning chemicals, particularly bleach with ammonia-based or acidic products, which can release toxic gases. Bleach also does nothing useful against petroleum stains.
          </p>

          <p>
            Runoff is the issue most people overlook. Wash water from oil stain cleaning contains oil and cleaner residue, and storm drains in most areas lead directly to local waterways. Many municipalities prohibit washing automotive fluids into them, and the EPA's stormwater guidance discourages it. Rinse onto gravel or lawn where permitted, block the drain with a berm or towels, or collect water with a wet vacuum.
          </p>

          <p>
            Used absorbent and poultice material holds the oil you removed. Small household amounts are generally accepted in regular trash once dry, but rules vary, and larger quantities belong at a household hazardous waste drop-off. Enzyme products are typically the least hazardous to handle and dispose of, which is part of their appeal for families with pets.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What an Oil Stain Really Costs to Remove
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sticker prices mislead because products treat very different amounts of stain. A gallon of degreaser concentrate at roughly $15 to $30 can make several gallons of working solution, enough for many stains and a whole-floor wash. A ready-to-use spray at a similar price may cover only a handful of spots. Poultice products at roughly $15 to $25 often treat two to four average stains per bottle, and you may need multiple rounds on each.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Approximate Cost per Average Stain</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Under $1</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Absorbent granules on a fresh spill</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$1-$3</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Degreaser concentrate, per round</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$5-$15</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Commercial poultice, per round</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$3-$10</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Enzyme powder, per treatment</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            These are rough ranges at the time of writing, based on typical package sizes and label coverage. The cheapest strategy is always speed: a fresh spill cleaned up with a few cents of absorbent and a squirt of degreaser costs almost nothing. A stain left for a year can need three poultice rounds or a two-week enzyme treatment, which adds up to $20 to $40 and several weekends.
          </p>

          <p>
            Professional cleaning is an option for large or numerous stains. Pressure washing companies with hot water equipment can often clean an entire driveway and treat oil spots in one visit. Prices vary widely by region and driveway size, so get quotes. For a single stain, DIY products are far cheaper; for a garage floor you plan to coat, professional prep is sometimes bundled into the coating price.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more cost to factor in is your time. A degreaser round takes about half an hour of active work. A poultice takes ten minutes to apply and ten to remove, spread over two days. An enzyme treatment takes a few minutes plus occasional misting over a week or more. If your weekends are scarce, the slower products are often the cheaper choice in practice, because they do most of their work while you are doing something else.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Household Alternatives: What Works and What Does Not
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Dish soap</strong> is a genuine degreaser, just a mild one. A generous amount scrubbed in and left for 15 minutes cleans fresh spills well, and it is safe on asphalt. On stains more than a few days old it lightens rather than removes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Baking soda</strong> is a mild absorbent and abrasive. It soaks up a little fresh oil and helps scrubbing, but it is less absorbent than clay granules. <strong>Kitty litter</strong>, the plain clay kind, is close to a commercial absorbent and is a perfectly good first response.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Cola</strong> gets recommended often because its phosphoric acid lifts a little surface grime, but it does not dissolve oil and leaves sugar behind that attracts dirt and insects. <strong>WD-40</strong> can loosen fresh grease, but it adds more petroleum that must then be cleaned off. <strong>Bleach</strong> lightens organic stains like mildew but has little effect on oil. <strong>Muriatic acid</strong> etches concrete and can leave a bright ring around an unchanged stain; it is not an oil remover.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Homemade poultice</strong> is the household method that genuinely competes with commercial products. Mix diatomaceous earth or finely ground clay litter with a strong degreaser into a thick paste, spread it a quarter inch thick, cover with plastic and leave it a day or two. It costs a few dollars and works on the same principle as bottled poultices.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Oven cleaner</strong> also turns up in online advice. Most aerosol oven cleaners are strongly alkaline, so they do break down grease, but they are expensive per square foot, harsh on skin and eyes, and can discolor some concrete. A gallon of alkaline degreaser concentrate does the same job more safely for less money. The honest summary is that dish soap, clay litter and a homemade poultice are worth using; most other kitchen-cabinet tricks are not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prevent" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Preventing the Next Stain
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once the concrete is clean and dry, seal it. A penetrating silane or siloxane sealer reduces how quickly oil soaks in without changing the look of the surface. It will not make concrete stainproof, but it buys time to blot a spill before it sets. Our guide to the <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">best concrete sealers</a> sorts products by surface and finish, and the <a href="/best-concrete-driveway-sealer-reviews/">concrete driveway sealer reviews</a> focus on exterior slabs.
          </p>

          <p>
            In a garage, a full coating offers the most protection. Epoxy and polyaspartic systems seal the floor completely, so oil sits on top and wipes away. They require thorough degreasing and surface preparation first, which is where the products above earn their keep.
          </p>

          <p>
            For cars that drip, a containment mat or drip pan is the cheapest insurance. Position it under the engine, not where you guess the engine sits. Check it periodically and get the leak fixed, because a steady drip will overwhelm any sealer over time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep a small kit by the garage door: absorbent granules, a stiff brush and a jug of degreaser. Most permanent stains began as small spills that sat because nothing was on hand. With the kit ready, a spill becomes a ten-minute chore instead of a weekend project.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, consider where cars actually park. Many driveway stains form in the same two or three spots, usually just inside the garage door and at the top of the driveway where a car waits. Treating and sealing those zones more often than the rest of the slab, and placing mats exactly there, gives most of the benefit of a full-driveway program for a fraction of the effort and cost.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a degreaser on a years-old stain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Degreasers only reach oil near the surface during their short dwell time. On a stain that has been in the slab for years, they clean the top and the stain returns as the concrete dries. Switch to a poultice or enzyme cleaner instead of buying a stronger degreaser.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the absorbent on fresh spills</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Putting cleaner straight on a puddle emulsifies the oil and spreads it over a larger area, sometimes driving it deeper. Pull the free oil up with granules first, grind them in, and wait several hours. Every product works better on a stain with the loose oil already removed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Rinsing an enzyme cleaner too soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Microbial products need time and moisture to digest oil. Rinsing after an hour, or letting the area dry out, stops them before they do much. Follow the label's no-rinse period, keep the spot damp, and wait at least a week before deciding it did not work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using concrete products on asphalt without checking</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Solvent-based and highly alkaline cleaners can soften asphalt binder or leave pale patches. Many driveway products assume concrete. Read the label for an asphalt statement, test a small corner first, and favor enzyme cleaners and absorbents on blacktop.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sending wash water into the storm drain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rinse water from stain removal carries oil and cleaner residue. Storm drains usually lead straight to streams, and many towns restrict what can go into them. Rinse onto gravel or lawn where allowed, berm off the drain, or collect the water with a wet vacuum.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which oil stain remover works best on concrete overall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the stain's age. For fresh spills, an absorbent like Oil-Dri followed by a degreaser like Oil Eater works best. For weeks-old stains, a poultice such as Pour-N-Restore. For old, faded stains, an enzyme product such as Terminator-HSD. Buying by stain age matters more than picking one brand.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do enzyme oil removers really work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They do on the right stain and with patience. Microbial cleaners break hydrocarbons down in place, so they reach oil that degreasers cannot. Owners commonly report gradual fading over one to two weeks. They work poorly in cold weather, on surfaces treated with bleach or acid, and on puddles that were not absorbed first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I mix two oil stain removers together?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Do not mix products in a bucket. Use them in sequence instead: absorbent, then degreaser, then a poultice or enzyme cleaner if needed. Rinse thoroughly between chemical products, especially before an enzyme treatment, because leftover degreaser residue can interfere with the microbes. Never combine any cleaner with bleach.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is TSP good for oil stains on concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Trisodium phosphate is a strong alkaline cleaner that degreases concrete effectively and is often used before painting. It works similarly to the alkaline degreasers above. It is caustic, so wear gloves and eye protection, and phosphates are restricted in some areas. Rinse well and keep it out of storm drains and plant beds.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many applications does a stain usually need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Fresh spills usually need one round of absorbent and degreaser. Stains a few weeks old often need two or three degreaser or poultice rounds. Old garage floor stains may need three to five poultice applications or a week or two of enzyme treatment. Judge each round only after the concrete is fully dry.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a pressure washer alone remove oil?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Cold water pressure washing rarely removes oil stains by itself, because water does not dissolve oil. It is excellent as a rinse after a degreaser or poultice, flushing emulsified oil out of the surface texture. Hot water units are much more effective on oil, which is why commercial cleaners use them.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is sealing worth it once the oil stains are gone?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, once the concrete is completely clean and dry. Check by sprinkling water: it should soak in evenly. A penetrating silane or siloxane sealer slows future oil absorption, giving you time to blot spills. Sealing over residual oil leads to poor adhesion, so finish the stain work first.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

